  {
    "episode_id": 84776,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "I mean, surely on the free end of that continuum, but not media enough, usually about the things we should be debating, but absolutely there was freedom to debate big things. I look at this moment and the silliness that's happening on the campuses, and I'm angry, but I'm also perplexed, but I'm also really kind of saddened. for the students. There seem to be so few adults at any of these institutions that it seems like people just starving for having actual meaningful conversations and debates and fights. And where I'm from where most everybody grows up working in the fields, it's just really hard to imagine people who've ever had to get up at 5 a.m. day after day to do real work. going off and thinking that any of the stuff that's happening at these colleges right now with these, you know, perceived grievances as real. It just seems both fluffy and shallow, and I'm sad for the students that don't even know what a real debate would look like.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
