    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Well, I mean, let's start with the idea that physical assault is an important word, right? I mean, violence is about physical action. We have a world where our politics are so confused right now, where Barney Frank can say and the Democratic National Committee and the run up to Obama's reelection can constantly reiterate these talking points that government is just another word for things we choose to do together. It just takes bizarrely vacuous sentence because no, it's not. Government is about power. It's about force. It's about being able to prohibit things and take your stuff or take your freedom. There's force and power and violence language that actually matters. And somehow, in a lot of these academic institutions, anything that could be considered mildly offensive or a threat to the worldview that somebody already held when they got there can be called violence. It's strange. And so obviously in your hypothetical, if you're at Dartmouth and students are going into the library and physically assaulting people, fortunately, have laws that protect people's bodies from physical assault.",
