  {
    "episode_id": 84776,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "sure so i'm ninety ninth and seniority in the senate seven forty three years old and never been in politics before uh... and david perdue and i had the only two people in the senate who've never uh... been involved in office seeking in our past And so I do speak a lot or had spoken a lot on the floor, but in the formal role of being president of the Senate. So under the Constitution, the vice president is of the United States, the president of the Senate, but rarely presides. So somebody gets assigned the duty of fitting in the chair and presiding over Senate debates. So I've spent a lot of time on the floor, had a lot of time to observe and learn. But I did intentionally wait a year to make a floor speech and gave that speech on the anniversary of my election day or one year out from next year's presidential election. And I did that for a couple of reasons. One, historically, the Senate used to have a tradition that freshmen didn't speak for their first year that you wanted to get to know the institution and youngins were meant to be seen and not heard and learned their way around. And I had pledged to Nebraska into my general election campaign that I plan to follow that. uh... tradition and the reason was that i've gone into a lot of broken places uh... over the last twenty years and knowing that a company is going bankrupt is different than understanding all the dysfunction and brokenness inside the company that are leading it to go bankrupt uh... knowing that the college doesn't have enough students like i took over a hundred thirty-year-old lutheran liberal arts college uh... six years ago and you know it which was shriveling up in lots of ways, economic to be sure, but also spiritual and intellectual and academic with what was going on at the school. And knowing why things are broken actually requires some humility and learning and discernment. And so I came into the Senate believing that this institution was pretty broken, but not thinking I necessarily knew all the reasons why. And so I wanted to spend a lot of that first year I'm interviewing members on both sides of the aisle and talking to people in private where there are no cameras and people posturing for soundbites or wanting to just be cry the other party. But talk about why the Senate has been on such a bad decades long decline as a venue for actually debating the big issues before us. And so that's what I want to spend my first year doing before I went out and tried to publicly articulate a theory of the brokenness.",
