    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 84776,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 33,
    "turn_text": "no cameras. People know that to be true, but it's really hard for a politician to admit that because it sounds like they're trying to say they don't want to be transparent. And I think it's a much broader problem about the differences between images and logic, flash words. I have no reticence about there being radio and audio, almost everywhere the Senate does its work. Cameras actually change it quite a lot. It isn't like the same thing happens once the cameras have been introduced, more people can just see it. It actually squelches a lot of the debate and the dialogue that might have happened. So things like that were kind of obvious and I knew them, I presumed them to be true and a lot of numbers confirmed that that is their judgment as well. Some things were really quite surprising. I think that the top line kind of national media liberal narrative about how fundraising corrupts the institution for instance, it's both really right and really wrong. There's this belief that there's way too much money in politics. Well, if you actually step back and look at the data, I think it's breathtaking how little money there is in politics compared to almost anything else.",
