  {
    "episode_id": 84776,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 45,
    "turn_text": "so at one level, it's not true that there's way too much money in politics. At another level, it is absolutely the case. that political officials and political figures spend more time trying to raise money than almost anything else. Well, that's an echo of this larger phenomenon of the obsession with incumbents trying to do politics all the time forever more. And that's sort of a stunning thing to experience. I believed this to be true before I got here, but it's still amazing to see how the biggest long-term factor in most conversations in Washington, BC, is the politician's own obsession with their next election. The long-term thinking of politics is driven by the long-term re-election and incumbency prospects. of those public figures and I think that real people who believed in this as public service would easily be able to admit that intellectually there may be moments where what the body politic needs may only be popular with forty five or forty eight percent of the people and if you stand for those things it might mean that in the short term you lose an election so what go back to your real life and if you believe in citizen governance that shouldn't be that scary but what drives the schedule around",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
