    "turn_idx": 57,
    "turn_text": "the old buckley line about uh... would rather be ruled by the first two thousand names in the boston phone book uh... then harvard faculty i sincerely believe that if you put together a list of the five hundred and thirty five graph tops officials in Nebraska that serve on not-for-profit boards or on the boards of small startup companies in Nebraska. Those 535 people are a heck of a lot more impressive than the 535 people that we have tried to run the legislative process for the United States government right now. And so we have huge selection problems in American politics. where it is so easy with a public that's disengaged and with as large a share of campaign money being spent on just lazy 30 second attack TV ads. Most people who are healthy and have a lot of good prospects in life don't want to think about running for office, because it seems like the way that you succeed is by really shallow, short-termist, soundbite culture, and a willingness to attack some other candidate in your term primary. And the odds of success seem small enough, and the likelihood that you're going to make a difference if you do succeed seems small enough, that lots of really good and healthy people don't run for office. And I think that's a crisis for our country.",
