    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 64,
    "turn_text": "uh... it is the inexorable growth of bureaucracies that seem to either be accountable to no one accountable to a president through a White House structure that is not possibly able to have the textured understanding of the diversity of 320 million people spread across 50 states. And so to me, the best way to start thinking about the administrative state is the experience I had with the rancher complaining about the waters of the U.S. rule that the EPA has recently promulgated and is losing on in court again and again Nebraska is the largest cattle state in the Union. And so if you're raising a bunch of cows and you're going through the early phases of their life all the way to the feedlot stages, there are a whole bunch of different kinds of habitats and environments and processes you use to grow a cow at 1,300 pounds. And at the end of a cow's life in the feedlot stage, if you have 10,000 cattle in a feedlot, the reality is you have a big manure pile. And when it rains, and rainwater runs off that manure pile, you can create negative externalities for the row crop and guy down the hill, because growing porn or beans or something like that. And there are environmental issues around that water that absolutely need to be managed. And the great news is we manage those environmental issues really, really effectively in Nebraska. Not even at the state level, we manage them at the county level. And we don't really need any help from unnamed bureaucrats at the EPA in Washington, BC telling us that they have new ideas how to manage rainwater runoff from a manure pile when they really don't have any idea what the issues even are. And so this rancher was complaining to me rightly about the waters of the US rule. And he said, he sort of paused and he looked up and he realized he'd been giving me a bunch of technical complaints about how the bureaucracy was working or not working. And he looked at me and he said, you know, I keep racking my brain. And no matter how hard I try, I just can't remember who I voted for at the EPA.",
