  },
  {
    "episode_id": 84776,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84776.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 80,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, if any small business or any not-for-profit kept accounting ledgers the way the federal government does, those directors and executives would all go to jail. So what Washington does is we have this screaming match every six to 18 months about the debt ceiling. And what the debt ceiling really is is it's the legal constraint on the Secretary of the Treasury's ability to sell more 30-year bonds to pay current obligations so when you know when someone goes into the hospital who's on medicare on the part a program and tries and you know they have some bill that comes into h h s health and even services in baltimore the secretary of h h s of the administrative cms they just send out money to pay that hospital that bill under the medicare program and then they call up the secretary of the treasury and say we just spent this money and you know check spiking about so we plan to put some of the account And so they go out and they sell 30-year treasury bonds, and that's how we pay for the shortfalls in the federal government every year in the deficit. It's a big deal. The number is now $19 trillion, which is really hard for any of us to actually get our heads around. But here's the much bigger story. That is a completely artificial constraint, the debt ceiling on the Secretary of the Treasury to be able to sell 30-year bonds. is just one thing, and the actual obligations to the federal government are roughly four times larger than that, we just keep them all off-book. So the difference between all future projected payroll tax revenues to the federal government and regular taxes as well, but payroll taxes, and so far as we're talking about these entitlement programs, and what the federal government has already promised to pay is more like $75 trillion than the $19 trillion number. And so there's just an action-forcing event around the debt ceiling that happens every six to 18 months. But most Americans don't know, and most politicians pay no attention to the fact that what the federal government actually owes is something like four times bigger than that,",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "If small businesses or non-profits kept accounting ledgers like the federal government, their directors and executives would go to jail.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The debt ceiling is a legal constraint on the Secretary of the Treasury's ability to sell 30-year bonds to cover current obligations.",
        "confidence": 0.94
