    "turn_text": "there's been you know for major kind of a economy in human history hunter gatherers settled agrarian farmers with their villages uh... the rise of cities urbanization industrialization and functionally big tool economies that people went and settled around and whatever we're entering now So we didn't have written alphabets when we went from hunter-gatherers to settled farmers, but we do know something about the disruptions of 1870 to 1940. Mass urbanization, immigration, industrialization, and folks were scared to death about whether or not the American experiment could really persist in the midst of all these old You know, Tocquevillian farmer villagers moving to big cities. What would this be like? What would community look like? What would self-discipline and self-control and self-governance look like? How does independence work in an era where people are moving to big cities and having to get jobs around these big factories? And fundamentally what that meant is 15 to 25-year-old males were mobile across the landscape. And 15 to 25-year-old males are always scary. And so people",
