    "turn_text": "into cities and the world is being highly disrupted, but it turned out it wasn't quite as scary as many writers and theoreticians and politicians at the time thought it might be because it turned out once you got from the farm to the city and you got connected to the new factory job, you were probably going to have that job until death or retirement. So there wasn't going to be constant disruption. There was this one really disrupted moment and it was scary. And think about all the you know, yinging and yanging in our political life with prohibition, and then the retraction of that. Many different things happen in American life about whether or not cities are going to be a place that you can have, tok-villain community. But once you have these jobs, you're stable. But the fears were understandable. We're entering the world now where that fear of disruption is probably our new normal forever. When I was a college president, I would read some of the demographers and economists about what would happen to the generation that was walking across our stage at graduation every May or June. And it wasn't just that they were going to change jobs. They were going to change industries three times between college graduation and age 30. Average duration at a firm now in the US is about 3.8 years. As recently as the Carter Reagan election, it still was pushing 30 years. And what's happening now is people's connection to their workplace is going to be disrupted at a much more rapid pace than ever before. And it probably doesn't end at some near term place. Technology will continue to disinimmediate lots of jobs or maybe another way to think about it. The uberization that we've seen of, you",
