    "turn_text": "Yeah, so I think Senator McCain's way of framing it as way behind is the best way to start. I think by analogy to other historical moments. So if you wanted to be getting in the Cold War to 1945 or 1948, You could ask yourself, well, heading to the mid-1950s, what is going to be the nature of Soviet expansionistic communism armed with nuclear tools? What is that going to look like? And what are we going to need to do to be prepared by 1955 or 1958? And then plot where we are on that pathway. Now fast forward to the present. When you spend time alone in a class of upsetting with intelligence leadership or with Pentagon brass, And you ask a question that I put to them all the time. What keeps you up at night? What are you afraid about right now? And especially if you put a time horizon on it. What are you worried about for 10 years from now? There is almost no one who ever answers that question with any divergence. They almost all say something about how cyber is going to transform the history and future of warfare and that we are not in any way prepared for it. We don't have an offensive cyber strategy. We don't have a defensive cyber strategy. And you could name hotspots around the world. We could talk about Putin's ambitions in general and in the particular moment of a lame doc Obama administration. You could talk about China's objectives in the South China Sea and with Pacific trade lines. You can talk about the chaos in the Middle East that's arguably unparalleled since 1917. And you can think of both state and non-state actors, and then you think of the kind of tools that are going to be available to folks in the era of cyber. And we are not in any kind of position like we were in, in terms of preparedness and clarity about what getting prepared will look like that we would have done in at the end of, at the end of World War II and the beginning of the Cold War. So Senator McCain's right, it's absolutely terrifying.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
