    "turn_idx": 109,
    "turn_text": "something like 22% of the adults, the voting age public, can even name either of their two senators. I don't care very much about you know, individual senators or individual politicians, egos. But just the idea that the public needs to own the small R republic. And if the public is going to own it and have responsibility for it and emotional, you know, big sold investment in the direction this country has headed, how can the public not be at all engaged in the questions that drive the future when we have, as you've directed us with your questioning today, Through entitlement budgets that are completely fake, the lack of a national security strategy for the age of cyber and jihad, the fact that we're going to go through massive disruption in the nature of work and what the economic implications of that are, we have huge policy challenges before us. And the public is so fundamentally disengaged, would the founders have believed that this kind of Republican, against small R, Republican experiment? could go forward. They had doubts about whether or not 4 million people could form one community. I mean, some of the federal structure of the Constitution is about the history of the 13 colonies, but it's also about the idea of localism and what engaged human public life looks like. And that's done as close to the people as possible. Can you go from 4 million to 320 million and have that 320 million be disengaged? I think our founders would be absolutely stunned by the turmoil of this moment. So I'm not meaning to exonerate the people for how messed up our public life is. But I also think one of the things the summer of 2015 tells us, the polling in the Republican primary in particular, is that the people wished there were a lever they could pull for none of the above. They wish there were other choices. They'd like to fire all 535 politicians and the only reason I push back as a little bit reticent about the term is because it's the professionalization of politics that I'm skeptical about. The over-specialization that implies that the only people who can help lead the country and make hard decisions are people who move to Washington and have a life that's centered on lobbying. I'm skeptical of that idea. I think we need a much, much broader share of the public engaged in these questions. And I think we would benefit from having lots more and different candidates than we currently have running. And so you can say when the public looks in the mirror and wants to point fingers, they should do it themselves as well. OK, I'll agree. But not just because of how they vote, but because not enough of them are running.",
