    "turn_text": "like the word base, but a set of voters who really believe because Republicans re-controlled or retook the House and the Senate, everything ought to be able to be changed in a second. I think both of those are bad options. The reality is Lobbyist-centric agendas in Washington, D.C., are ever going to rise to the level of vision that the people want about an America where the meaning of this country is so much bigger than the particular powers of individual federal bureaucracies at any given moment in time. People want a vision. of freedom and of a constitutional system that says most everything that's meaningful in life happens in civic institutions and in not-for-profits and in small businesses and in churches and in families. That's just true. That's just anthropologically accurate about where life is well lived. And so I think that you have to be able to have more politicians that can articulate a meaning of America that's bigger than particular federal powers. And so being small ball centric on DC law, being even good enough, it's also not okay though to reduce the legislative task to whatever you can get done in legislation in the next two months or, you know, 24 months of an electoral cycle. And that impulse is leading a lot of people to wish that the Senate were a simple majoritarian body. You hear this on conservative talk radio a lot. Harry Reid did all these terrible things by invoking the nuclear option, so why don't Republicans show that they have the cajones to invoke the nuclear option as well and make the Senate a simple, majoritarian body? I think that's a really, really short-sighted way to think about the problem, because if you look at the biggest pieces of federal policy that are leading us in bad directions, they fundamentally emanate from three historical moments, the mid-1930s. the mid-1960s and 2009 and 10, what are those three moments in time having common? In all three cases, the Democrats had more than 60 votes, and so they had super majoritarian powers to get big, bad things done",
