    "turn_idx": 126,
    "turn_text": "is to defend us, to defend life and property and to protect us against enemies, foreign and domestic. And you have to have some sense of where the government's responsibilities begin. And I don't really ever find anyone who looks at a baby in utero. one week or one month before birth and says, yeah, that's not a life. That's not worthy of respect. It's not our responsibility to protect it. And so I think we look at those little baby boys and girls and I think it's one of our responsibilities to try to protect them. Obviously, it's a hugely contentious issue. And so I would prefer, as you just tried to do, to try to take some of the heat out of the conversation and try to put a little more light and logic on it and try to start by working on those issues that have the most potential to actually move people. I think there are a lot of national political ideologues on both sides that really take anything related to the culture wars and think that all of the issues move in lockstep. I don't think that that's actually true on the ground and so even though many things are happening in the culture wars that give me a lot of pause I think that on the issue of whether or not a baby that's still in Mom's tummy is a human being with dignity, created in God's image and worthy of respect and protection, I actually think that issue is moving in the right direction because this younger generation is arguably more pro-life than any generation at its moment in time in the many decades that we've been having this fight and there's a lot wrong with being an image image-centric culture, but one of the good things is people are seeing pictures of little babies, and they're increasingly recognizing them to be little babies, and so I think that's good news.",
