    "turn_text": "of the worst season in a century plus of Nebraska football. So there's a lot of mourning happening in our state. It's the second most important religion in Nebraska. And so we're feeling pain about it. But it's kind of interesting to think about Nebraska as the winning college football program from 1960 to about 2010 in the nation. One five national championships over that period at one point went 62 and three over five years only lost to three games. Really some amazing statistics that you could pile up. And yet by far the most important moment in the history of Nebraska football and any fan from the most devoted to the most casual in our state would say that it was January 2nd, 1984, where Nebraska under Coach Osborne is playing Miami. We're number one in the nation. We're in the Orange Bowl. They're number two in the nation and for a good part of that 1983 season. um, sports illustrated had ranked Nebraska's second string as the number two nation in the country. Because Nebraska was so good, they'd have to pull all their starters by the middle of the second quarter and you'd put in this other group that effectively got a half of the game in themselves and they would dominate anybody else in the country even if they were ranked fifth or tenth in the country. And so we're in the National Championship game and Miami gets a big lead and Nebraska has to come back and at the end of the game we score a touchdown with the overwhelming number one team. There's no overtime back then. There's no two-point conversion. I'm sorry, there was a two-point conversion. There's no extended time. And Osborne, without missing a beat, when all you have to do is kick the point to tie the game and win the national championship. Osborne instantly, without a second hesitation, puts two fingers in the air. And nobody in the state had any doubt that that was the right move. We end up missing the two-point conversion, losing the game 31 to 30, finishing second in the nation, don't win the national championship. And the entire state believed that that was the right choice because what honor and dignity and getting up at 5 a.m. in the morning to raise crops and to feed your cattle or to break the ice off the top of the water so your cattle can drink what it means is you had a duty that day, you had a job. And Nebraska was supposed to go out and play Miami. They're not supposed to be playing for the polls and for national championships. They're supposed to beat Miami today. And the way you try to beat Miami is by going for two. And when you think about the state of only, you know, now 1.85 million people and that kind of consensus, it actually says something about localism and dignity and",
